AD  - Department of Surgery, University of Chicago, Chicago, IL, United States
AD  - Department of Surgery, Pediatric Surgery, University of Chicago, Chicago, IL, United States
AB  - Splenic conditions in children requiring splenectomy include Hereditary Spherocytosis, Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura, and sickle cell disease. Laparoscopic splenectomy has largely replaced open splenectomy. Laparoscopic energy devices and staplers allow easy and secure division of all the ligamentous and vascular attachments of the spleen. Care must be taken to evaluate for the presence of accessory spleens, as they can lead to postoperative symptom recurrence if left in place. Partial splenectomy and single-incision laparoscopic splenectomy can also be performed in select cases and reflect the ongoing evolution of technical modifications. Complications from splenectomy are relatively rare in children but can be life-threatening when present. Intraoperative bleeding and difficulties achieving hemostasis remain a concern and may require conversion to an open procedure. Splenic and portal vein thrombosis have been reported in children, though much less commonly than in adult patients. The primary long-term concern is overwhelming post-splenectomy infection, a potentially fatal complication of splenectomy for which young children are at increased risk. Vaccination and antibiotic prophylaxis appear to be the most effective in preventing post-splenectomy sepsis. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
