AB  - This chapter focuses on the management of dogs presenting with acute gastric dilatation and volvulus (GDV). Dogs with GDV have rotation of the stomach, typically clockwise and between 180 and 360 degrees. Once rotation has occurred, a number of local and systemic effects result. Stabilization of dogs with GDV should be rapid and performed prior to anesthesia and surgery. Surgical treatment of GDV consists of several distinct steps that must be performed to ensure the best outcome for the dog: decompression and repositioning of the stomach; assessment of gastric and splenic viability; resection of devitalized tissue; and gastropexy. The initial aim of surgery is to decompress and reposition the stomach. Once the stomach is decompressed and repositioned, the gastric wall should be evaluated for evidence of necrosis. The spleen is closely associated with the greater curvature of the stomach and, as a result, splenic torsion is common in dogs with GDV. © 2022 John Wiley & Sons, Inc.
