AD  - Department of Geriatric Surgery, National Clinical Research Center for Geriatric Disorders, Xiangya Hospital, Central South University, Changsha, China
AB  - Over the last few years, there has been much evolution in minimally invasive surgical techniques, resulting in less pain, shorter hospital stay, and better cosmetic results without compromising patient safety. The laparoscopic technique for splenectomy has become the preferred method for splenectomy at many institutions. In recent decades, as constantly revised and updated medical knowledge has broadened our horizons and a less invasive method named natural orifice specimen extraction surgery (NOSES) is catching substantial attention world and wide. NOSES is a step forward in making laparoscopic surgery even less invasive. NOSES is featured with the removal of surgical specimen from natural orifice in the avoidance of abdominal incision, which has been considered as an alternative approach to open surgery and conventional laparoscopic surgery in selected patients and feasibility of NOSES is well documented. The route of surgical NOSES for specimen removal is mainly through the vagina and anus. Laparoscopic splenectomy with transvaginal specimen extraction is mainly applied to spleen (splenic tumors), idiopathic or HIV-related thrombocytopenic purpura, hematological hemolytic anemia, splenic cyst, wandering spleen, lymphoma, leukemia, etc. The main surgical procedures include: (1) laparoscopic splenectomy and (2) transvaginal removal of specimens. For suitable patients, laparoscopic splenectomy and transvaginal removal of specimen without auxiliary incision in the abdomen is safe and feasible. The advantages of NOSES include a shortened postoperative hospitalization, improved cosmesis, and less analgesic requirements. © People’s Medical Publishing House, PR of China 2023.
