AB  - The spleen is a large, encapsulated, complex mass of vascular and lymphoid tissue situated in the upper left quadrant of the abdominal cavity between the fundus of the stomach and the diaphragm. The spleen captures the old or abnormal red blood cells and foreign bodies to filtrate the blood in the red pulp. The spleen is involved in immune reactions in the white pulp, including the marginal zone. The spleen in the fetus is also a major site of haemopoiesis and can resume this role postnatally in certain pathological conditions. The splenic primordium germinates as several nodules in the mesogastrium which connect the stomach and the posterior abdominal wall and connects to one. The lobulation of the spleen results from the nodules and the incomplete adhesion of the splenic nodules causes the accessory spleen. The spleen moves to the left upper abdomen with the rotation of the stomach. This makes two peritoneal ligaments connecting the splenic hilum with the stomach and posterior abdominal wall, the gastrosplenic ligament and the splenorenal ligament. The blood vessels run in these peritoneal ligaments. Excessive extension of the ligaments during abdominal surgery causes damage and loosening of the ligaments causes mobile spleen. © 2021 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
