AB  - Splenic masses are found by palpation, radiography or ultrasonography (the preferred technique). A splenectomy is indicated for tumours, splenic torsion or large haematomas. Complications of splenectomy are haemorrhage and cardiac problems. A haemangiosarcoma should be suspected when there is abdominal haemorrhage associated with a splenic mass. Abdominal and cardiac ultrasonography is indicated. The most common splenic masses are haemangiosarcoma, nodular hyperplasia and haematoma in dogs, and mastocytoma and lymphosarcoma in cats. In dogs, about a quarter of splenic masses are haemangiosarcomas and half of these are not malignant and therefore have a better prognosis.
