AB  - A case of chronic splenic necrosis in a six-year old male Bernese mountain dog is described. A thorough clinical evaluation, including ultrasonography, radiography, radiology and laboratory tests, were performed. The results suggested a neoplastic disease of the spleen, but exploratory laparotomy revealed a non-neoplastic, kidney-shaped spleen which was twisted on its mesentery. Splenectomy was performed and, following eight days of intensive supportive postoperative treatment, the animal was euthanised because recovery from surgery was not visible. Histologically, the splenic architecture was completely destroyed with necrosis and haemorrhage throughout. The findings indicate that the torsion had existed longer than was estimated from the initial examination.
