AB  - Torsion of a wandering spleen is a very rare condition. The clinical appearance is nonspecific. Imaging findings play an important role in the diagnosis. In this report, we describe a 39-year-old woman with splenic torsion in whom the diagnosis was established with computed tomography (CT) and color Doppler sonography (CDS). Computed tomography showed the typical whorled appearance of the ectopic splenic pedicle, and CDS revealed a hypoechoic enlarged pelvic spleen with no flow in the parenchyma or vascular pedicle. The intrahepatic portal vein was also thrombosed with minimal peripheral flow. During laparotomy, an enlarged and infarcted pelvic spleen was found and removed surgically. © 2005 by the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine.
