TI  - Splenic torsion requiring splenectomy six years following laparoscopic nissen fundoplication
PY  - 2012
T2  - Journal of the Society of Laparoendoscopic Surgeons
VL  - 16
IS  - 1
SP  - 184
EP  - 188
DO  - 10.4293/108680812X13291597716861
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84866162263?origin=resultslist
AD  - South East Area Health Education Center, Department of Surgery, New Hanover Regional Medical Center, Wilmington, NC, United States
AB  - Background: Laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication has become a mainstay in the surgical treatment of gastroesophageal reflux disease, as it has proved to be a durable, well-tolerated procedure. Despite the safety and efficacy associated with this procedure, surgeons performing this advanced laparoscopic surgery should be well versed in the potential intraoperative and postoperative complications. Methods: A case is presented of a rare complication of splenic torsion following laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication. Diagnostic evaluations and intraoperative findings are discussed. Results: We present an otherwise healthy 41-year-old woman who underwent a laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication 6 years earlier at another medical center and presented with worsening chronic left upper quadrant abdominal pain. She was diagnosed with torsion of the splenic vascular pedicle, resulting in heterogenicity of perfusion with associated hematoma requiring open splenectomy. Conclusion: Surgeons should be aware of splenic torsion as a potential, albeit rare, complication related to laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication. © 2012 by JSLS, Journal of the Society of Laparoendoscopic Surgeons.
