TI  - Clinicopathological findings, molecular detection and characterization of Babesia gibsoni infection in a sick dog from Italy
PY  - 2009
T2  - Veterinary Parasitology
VL  - 165
IS  - 3-4
SP  - 318
EP  - 322
DO  - 10.1016/j.vetpar.2009.07.022
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/70349780407?origin=resultslist
AD  - Laboratorio d'Analisi Veterinarie San Marco, 35141 Padua, Via Sorio, 114c, Italy
AD  - Ematos Vet Lab, Rome, Italy
AD  - Department of Pathology and Infectious Diseases, Royal Veterinary College, University of London, United Kingdom
AB  - A 4-year-old intact female American Pit Bull Terrier from Italy descendant of an American-born bitch was evaluated for anorexia, lethargy, weakness, and intermittent vomiting. On physical examination, the dog was dehydrated, had pale mucous membranes, hunched posture and abdominal pain. A moderate anemia was observed. Splenomegaly and hyperechoic regions suspected as infarcts in the spleen were seen on abdominal ultrasound. Based on the suspicion of splenic torsion, splenectomy was performed. After surgery, the clinical condition deteriorated. A follow-up complete blood count demonstrated severe macrocytic normochromic anemia with evidence of marked regeneration, left shift neutrophilia, monocytosis and marked thrombocytopenia. Blood smear evaluation revealed single to multiple, variable sized (1-3 μm in diameter), and round to oval to band-like piroplasms within many red blood cells consistent with small form Babesia spp. or Theileria spp. A partial segment of the 18S rRNA gene was amplified and the PCR product was analyzed by direct sequencing. The nucleotide sequence was completely identical to that of Babesia gibsoni present in GenBank®. This is the first molecular detection and characterization of B. gibsoni infection in a sick dog from Italy. © 2009 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
