AB  - We report a case of Treacher Collins syndrome (TCS) with novel craniofacial and extracraniofacial findings. A male infant, product of an uncomplicated pregnancy, delivered at 37 weeks to a 34-year-old gravid 3 para 0, TA2 mother of Caribbean origin and a Dutch Caucasian father, was noted to have craniofacial anomalies. Soon after delivers he developed respiratory distress followed by difficulties establishing an airway. His birth weight was 2.505 kg (below the 5th centile). The head circumference was 35.5cm (above the 95th centile). Several novel features were noted of the craniofacial region (Figs 1-3, see also the autopsy findings). In addition, he had displaced thyroid and thymus, hypogonadism, cardiac anomalies, an accessory spleen and ectopic adrenal tissues. Molecular testing revealed a frame-shift mutation Nt4365 delA that resulted in a read-through of the native stop codon of the TCOFI gene. In addition, a novel missense variant Nt 1535 T > C was noted that changed a methionine to a threonine. The mother also has this variant and her facial radiographs (Waters projection) were normal. © 2009 Wolters Kluwer Health | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins.
