TI  - Detection of Splenosis and Ectopic Spleens with 99mTc‐Labelled Heat Damaged Autologous Erythrocytes in 90 Splenectomized Patients
PY  - 1981
T2  - Scandinavian Journal of Haematology
VL  - 27
IS  - 1
SP  - 51
EP  - 56
DO  - 10.1111/j.1600-0609.1981.tb00451.x
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0019401766?origin=resultslist
AD  - University Department of Medicine and Haematology, Aarhus Amtssygehus, Denmark
AD  - Department of Nuclear Medicine, Aarhus Kommunehospital, Aarhus, Denmark
AB  - Splenosis or ectopic spleens were detected in 22 of 45 patients splenectomized after either abdominal trauma or accidental lesions of the spleen during operation. The incidence of ectopic spleen tissue in various groups of splenectomized patients has been investigated by a sensitive scanning method employing reinjection of 99mTc‐labelled heat damaged autologous erythrocytes. In comparison 7 cases were found among 45 patients who underwent splenectomy for haematological reasons. The time span between the operation and a positive scan varied between 3 months and 11 years. None of the patients in the haematological group with reoccurrence of spleen tissue presented any signs of relapse of their primary disorder. The only patient with overwhelming infection was a girl in whom splemectomy was performed for hereditary spherocytosis. She recovered from the sepsis and her scan was negative. It is concluded that recurrence of spleen tissue is frequent after traumatic lesions of the spleen but rare after selective splenectomy for haematological reasons. This may account for the lesser tendency to overwhelming sepsis after post‐traumatic splenectomy. © Munksgaard 1981
