AB  - A 95-year-old female was found to have an asymptomatic pelvic mass on a routine gynecological exam. Ultrasound demonstrated a solid pelvic mass with a configuration suggesting the spleen. This was confirmed by Tc-99m sulfur colloid liver-spleen imaging. Ectopic pelvic spleens are very rare and occur primarily in women. Agenesis or laxity of the suspensory ligaments of the spleen are the most frequent causes. Many patients with an ectopic or wandering pelvic spleen are symptomatic early in life, and occasionally an acute abdomen may result from torsion of the splenic pedicle. The mass effect of a pelvic spleen may also interfere with normal pregnancy. This case demonstrates, however, that an ectopic pelvic spleen can remain asymptomatic into adult life and even advanced age, and may mimic malignancy. Although ultrasound may suggest the diagnosis, liver-spleen scintigraphy is diagnostic.
