AD  - Department of Radiology, St. Marianna Univ. Sch. of Medicine, Kawasaki-city, Kanagawa 216-8511, 2-16-1 Sugao, Miyamae-ku, Japan
AB  - Torsion and volvulus are congenital or acquired twists of organs. Although these conditions are potentially life threatening, their clinical pictures are often atypical, especially in pediatric or geriatric patients. The clue to reaching the correct diagnosis is to discover the precise organ that is twisted and to search for evidence of vascular compromise of that organ. This article reviews the imaging characteristics of a wide variety of twists in the abdomen, including adnexal, testicular, gallbladder, omental, and splenic torsion, as well as gastric volvulus, intestinal malrotation with midgut volvulus, and small bowel, cecal, and sigmoid volvulus.
