AB  - The authors report a new case of peritoneal splenosis. This disease is characterized by grafts of splenic tissue on the serosa of intra abdominal organs: the jejunum in this case. They are usually discovered 10 yr after splenectomy for rupture of the spleen, during laparotomy for abdominal pain resembling appendicitis, or during an operation for another disease. Histologically, these grafts have the structure of splenic tissue but differ by the absence of a hilum. They are nourished by peripheral neovessels and are not functional, which distinguishes them from ectopic spleen. It is usually impossible to remove them, due to their large number; biopsy of a nodule is all that is necessary for diagnosis.
