UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0017101715?origin=resultslist
AD  - United States
AB  - Heterotropic autotransplantation of splenic tissue following splenectomy in experimental animals results in reorganizations of the implant matrix (after initial necrosis of cellular elements) into splenic tissue that is microscopically indistinguishable from the original spleen. Chronically splenectomized animals reflect depressed levels of serum opsonins and homocytotropic immunoglobulins which parallels depressed macrophage function. Recent reports from this laboratory revealed that these defects could be corrected in splenectomized rats bearing ectopic splenic tissue. The present work was designed to substantiate the ectopic spleen as a site of immunoglobulin production. The pattern of direct PFC counts from reorganized splenic tissues paralleled PFC exhibited by cells from splenic implants and intact spleen exhibited respectively 40.5% and 43.6% inhibition of migration. Repeated studies gave similar results. These experiments indicate that reorganized ectopic autotransplantation of splenic tissue exhibits immune functions towards particulate antigens that are similar to normal spleen. Previous studies have indicated that they are morphologically functional as they can trap red cells and perform erythrophagocytosis. They are also able to ward off infections due to Bartonella muris that are fatal to splenectomized rats and may be clinically significant in the prevention of fatal sepsis in splenectomized children.
