AB  - Location of the spleen in a position other than the left upper quadrant is uncommon, and is the most often brought to the physician's attention when spleen simulates a mass or undergoes torsion. The etiology of wandering spleen is unknown. Incomplete fusion of the posterior mesogastrium, and failure of development of supporting ligaments may play a role. The condition is most often seen in multiparous females, often during or after the pregnancy. In this paper, we describe a young woman with suprapubic tumor and no discomfort. The diagnosis was established by scintigraphy and arteriography of the spleen, after ultrasonographic examination of the tumor. Histologically, the splenic tissue was normal. Splenectomy represents the treatment of choice.
